\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The orthogonal comparison of selected precisions}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The \newvocab{orthogonal comparison} of selected precisions is
##{(Q,P)\sim_O(Q',P')}
when there is an orthogonal isomorphism #{O:H\to H'} with
#{Q'=OQO^{-1}} and #{P'=OPO^{-1}}. It is an equivalence relation
by identity, inverse and composition. Every
\vocabk{background arrow}{fdip-005C} supplies such a comparison. The same
proof transports the selected law by #{O|_X}, but an arbitrary #{O}
need not transport vertex or edge labels. This relation differs both
from the [scaled Laplacian relation](fdip-003H), which forgets mass
and selector, and from [general linear transport](fdip-003M), which
may change the specified metric.}

\p{The [cospectral graph example](fdip-005H) proves that this comparison
can identify selected backgrounds with nonisomorphic graphs. Their
[graph and orthogonal stabilizers](fdip-005I) also carry different
information.}
